In Java, the `==` operator is used to compare the reference of two objects, not their values. When you use `==` to compare strings, Java is actually checking if the two strings are the same object in memory, not if they have the same value.

On the other hand, the `equals()` method is used to compare the values of two strings. When you call `.equals()` on a string object, Java compares the sequence of characters in the two strings, starting from the first character, until it finds a difference or reaches the end of both strings.

In your case, you encountered a bug because you were comparing two different string objects, even though they contained the same sequence of characters. By using `.equals()` instead of `==`, you were comparing the values of the strings, which is what you intended to do.

So, when should you use `==` and when should you use `.equals()`?

Use `==` to compare references of primitive types (int, double, boolean, etc.) and references of objects that are immutable (String literals, for example). Use `.equals()` to compare the values of mutable objects, such as String objects.

Here's a summary:

- Use `==` to compare references of primitive types and string literals.
- Use `.equals()` to compare the values of mutable objects, such as String objects.

Here's an example to illustrate the difference:

```java
String str1 = "hello";
String str2 = "hello";
String str3 = new String("hello");

// str1 == str2 is true, because they are the same string literal in memory

str3 = str3.toUpperCase();

// str1 == str3 is false, because they are different objects in memory
// But str1.equals(str3) is true, because their values are equal
```

To check if a number is a power of three without using loops or recursion, we can use the bit manipulation technique. The idea is to keep shifting the bits of the number by one position and check if the number is divisible by three after each shift. If at any point the number becomes zero or the shift count exceeds the log base three of the maximum possible number, then the number is not a power of three.

Here's the Java code for the same:

```java
public boolean isPowerOfThree(int n) {
    if (n <= 0) return false;
    int power = 1;
    while (n > 1) {
        if (n % 3 != 0) return false;
        power *= 3;
        n /= 3;
    }
    return n == power;
}

// Optimized version using bit manipulation
public boolean isPowerOfThree(int n) {
    if (n <= 0) return false;
    return (Long.valueOf(1L << (32 * Integer.SIZE - 2)) & n) == n && Integer.bitCount(n) % 2 == 0;
}
```

The first method checks if the number is a power of three by repeatedly dividing it by three and checking the remainder. The second method uses bit manipulation to check if the number is a power of three. It checks if the last bit of the number is set and if the number of set bits is even. This is because a power of three has a 1 in its last bit and has an even number of 1s in its binary representation.

